## Goal
Show how the treated (B1-B3) and untreated (A1-A3) samples separate on a principal component
analysis of the gene-level counts: whether the two groups occupy distinct regions of PC space,
and how much variance each leading component explains. This is exploratory and descriptive;
it runs no statistical test and draws no conclusion about differential expression.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper (native) | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets (native) | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

The count matrix is genes x samples (`gene_id`, `gene_name`, then A1 A2 A3 B1 B2 B3); values
are length-scaled and non-integer. The design supplies `sample_id` -> `condition`
(untreated: A1-A3, treated: B1-B3). Both are read-only.

## Method
Environment: `gars-bio` (pandas, numpy, scikit-learn, matplotlib; no installs). One script,
`scripts/sample_pca.py`, takes the two input paths and the output directory as arguments.

1. Read the count matrix and the design. Check that the matrix's sample columns and the
   design's `sample_id` values are the same set; stop with an error if not. Order samples as
   in the design.
2. Drop genes with a total count below 10 across all six samples (removes unexpressed genes).
3. Transform: `log2(count + 1)`. The counts are already length-scaled, so no further
   length or library-size normalisation is applied.
4. Select the 500 genes with the highest variance across samples on the log2 scale (all
   retained genes if fewer than 500).
5. Centre each gene to mean zero; do not scale to unit variance.
6. PCA with `sklearn.decomposition.PCA` (full SVD, `n_components` = min(6, n_samples)).
7. Write the per-sample PC scores and the explained-variance table.
8. Plot PC1 vs PC2, one point per sample, coloured by `condition`, labelled with sample ID,
   axis labels carrying percent variance explained. A second panel shows the scree
   (variance explained per component).

No input is modified. Parameters in steps 2, 4 and 5 are fixed here; changing them is a new
analysis.

